---
kind: instructions
title: "Task Files (one per plan)"
summary: "Task files: the executable checklist for one plan, one phase section per phase."
---

`context/tasks/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<slug-plan>.md` holds the task list for a
**plan**, with one `## Phase <n>` section per phase. One file per plan is the
default so a sequentially executed plan does not multiply files; split into
several files only to divide the work by context or across sub-agents (disjoint
scopes, each with a `--stream <label>` suffix). The task file is the
**execution unit**: work happens from one task file — and one phase section — at
a time, never from the plan.

## Create

Create the plan's task file with the CLI (scaffolds the path and frontmatter):

```bash
sdt context task add "<step>" [--phase <n>] [--stream <label>] [--plan <plan-file>]
```

`--phase <n>` places the step in the `## Phase <n>` section (created when
absent); without it the step lands in the file's unphased checklist. `--stream`
selects an opt-in split file (`<slug-plan>-<label>.md`). Timestamps
(`created`/`updated`) come from `sdt time iso` (RFC3339 UTC). Or hand-write the
file from the template below.

## Intent first

Task files follow the plan, which follows the analysis; the chain and its
approval gates are defined in AGENTS.md (HARD RULE 1).

A task file is owned by its plan: it carries the optional `phases` list (the
phase sections it covers; omitted on the whole-plan file) and **inherits the
plan's `objective`** for grouping — it never declares `objective` itself.

**One home for the checklist.** The task file's `## Phase <n>` checklist is the
**single home** of the executable steps. The plan carries each phase's intent and
approach (the "how"); it never repeats the checklist. A plan phase and its task
`## Phase` section must not restate the same list — the plan states *what and
how*, the task states the *executable steps*.

**Subject resolution (`>tasks`, `>execute`).** Both triggers are **workflow**
commands. `>tasks`'s subject is a `plan`; `>execute`'s subject is a `plan` and
one of its task files. They resolve it through the working-context ladder
(`context/commands/index.md`, *Working-context resolution*): explicit payload →
exactly one active/unique candidate → the plan under discussion → **ask**. When
`>execute` finds a plan with no task file, it stops and points to `>tasks` (task
creation is gated on plan approval); it never invents a task file.

## Structure

```markdown
---
kind: tasks
summary: "<1-2 sentence summary — MANDATORY, index source>"
phases: [1, 2]           # optional: only on a split file; omitted on the whole-plan file
status: pending          # pending | in-progress | completed | archived | active(legacy) — statuses defined in the matrix (architecture/stack.md)
created: "<ISO 8601>"
updated: "<ISO 8601>"
links:
  - plan/<date>-<plan-slug>.md
sources:
  - plan/<date>-<plan-slug>.md
project: <project>
agent: <agent/tool>          # optional
model: <model id>            # optional
session: <session id>        # optional
---

## Design

- <analysis objective this task covers> — approach: <how>;
  verify: <command or signal>
- <analysis objective this task covers> — approach: <how>;
  verify: <command or signal>

## Phase 1 — <goal>

- [ ] step one <!-- c1 -->
- [~] step two (in progress) <!-- c2 -->

## Phase 2 — <goal>

- [x] step three (done) <!-- c3 -->
- [!] step four (blocked) <!-- c4 -->

## Deviations             <!-- one item per deliberate departure from the plan -->

- [ ] fix — the phase order was wrong; corrected <!-- c7 -->
- [ ] stop-and-ask — the dependency choice needs the user's decision <!-- c8 -->

## Review                 <!-- file-level; written by `task review` on completion -->

### Findings             <!-- one item per finding; the verdict is the trailing token -->

- [ ] <claim> — CONFIRMED <!-- c5 -->
- [ ] <claim> — UNVERIFIED <!-- c6 -->

### Disposition          <!-- what kind of gap the review found -->

<CONTEXT-GAP | JUDGMENT-GAP | NONE>

### Gate                 <!-- appended by `agent gate --record`; a failed run says so -->

2026-01-01T00:00:00Z (sdt agent gate) — passed
- build: pass (1.20s)
- vet: pass (0.40s)
- lint: pass (3.00s)
- test: pass (11.00s)
```

The trailing `<!-- c<N> -->` is a stable per-document id the CLI assigns; never
type or renumber it by hand.

## Design section (study of the task objectives)

Before executing, add a `## Design` section: one short block per objective the
task's phases cover. Each block names the objective (from the plan phase and the
analysis it derives from), the approach/design chosen for it, and how it will be
verified. Keep it short — it studies *this task's* objectives; the full style,
architecture and dependency contract lives in the plan (see
`instructions/plan.md`), so do not duplicate it here.

## Checklist item detail

Each checklist item should be a single, self-contained action. For items that
need more context, use a description indented below the checkbox:

```markdown
- [ ] Refactor auth middleware to use token pool
      File: `internal/auth/middleware.go` — replace per-request
      token allocation with a sync.Pool; must not change the public API.
- [~] Add unit tests for token pool
      Must cover: concurrent access, pool exhaustion fallback.
      Verification: `go test ./internal/auth/ -race`
- [ ] Update migration script
      Depends on: refactor auth middleware.
```

Keep descriptions concise: what changes, which files, what to verify. Avoid
full paragraphs — the task file's Verification commands and Definition of Done
sections handle that.

Each item gets a trailing `<!-- c<N> -->` id when the CLI writes it; the anchor
is invisible when rendered and lets `sdt context check`/`task <verb>` address the
item without depending on its position. An item is a checklist line plus its
indented continuation lines; the id is written on the checklist line and is
**unique per document** (ids are not per phase), so do not add, edit, reorder or
reuse an anchor by hand — `--phase` selects the file, it does not scope an id
search. A repeated id in one document is a `sdt context lint` WARNING, and
`sdt context checklist backfill` renumbers duplicates.

## Frontmatter properties

| Property | Mandatory | Description |
|----------|-----------|-------------|
| kind | yes | Document type, fixed value `tasks`; index and lint depend on it. |
| summary | yes | 1-2 sentence summary → index source. |
| phases | no | Phase sections this file covers (e.g. `[1, 2]`); only on a split file, omitted on the whole-plan file. The legacy scalar `phase` is still accepted. The task inherits the plan's `objective`; it does not carry one. |
| status | yes | File state: `pending` (to work on) / `in-progress` / `completed` / `archived`. The first three are always distinguishable — a task file is never stuck with fewer than three meaningful states. `active` is the legacy value accepted by lint. |
| created | yes | ISO 8601 UTC creation date (use `sdt time iso`). |
| updated | yes | ISO 8601 UTC last-edit date, written only by the CLI; refresh a body-only edit with `sdt context touch`. |
| links | yes | Relative path to the plan this task file executes. |
| project | yes | Project id from `.sdt.yaml` (e.g. `sdt_01a2b3c4`). |
| agent | no | Agent/tool that picked up or last edited the file. |
| model | no | Model id of that agent — provenance. |
| session | no | Session id for traceability across edits. |

## Rules

- **No H1 title** — see AGENTS.md (document conventions).
- **CLI-only state** — never hand-edit `status:`, `updated:` or a checkbox
  marker. Mutate them with the CLI: `sdt context task <sub> --phase <n>`
  (add/list/done/block/wip/review) or, for any checklist, `sdt context check
  <doc> <id> --done|--wip|--block|--pending`; refresh a body-only edit with
  `sdt context touch <doc>`.
- Status markers: `[ ]` todo · `[~]` in-progress · `[x]` done · `[!]` blocked.
- File status transitions: create → `pending`; `wip`/`block` → `in-progress`; `done` → `completed` when no `[ ]`/`[~]` item remains, else `in-progress`. Completion is `completed` and the file stays in `context/tasks/`; `archived` is reserved for a task file retired by the user's will.
- **One file per plan by default**: the file holds the plan's phases as `## Phase
  <n>` sections. Split into several files only to divide the work by context or
  across sub-agents — disjoint scopes, each named with a `--stream <label>` and
  declaring its `phases` list. A plan phase with no matching section in any of
  its task files gets a `sdt context lint` SUGGESTION.
- Keep each phase section small (~5-7 items); a >10-item section triggers a
  `sdt context lint` SUGGESTION to split the phase.
- Task files are **living**: updated during execution of the phase.
- When every phase section is done and the `## Review` block is present, set the
  file `completed` (it stays in `context/tasks/`) and update the plan.

## Execution workflow

1. **Pick** — choose a task file whose dependencies are satisfied (see its plan
   and links). One file at a time; don't jump between files. Concurrent
   sub-agents must own different files (disjoint scopes).
2. **Take in charge** — before any work, mark the phase in progress with
   `sdt context task wip <id> --phase <n>` (it sets the item `[~]`, flips the
   file `in-progress` and refreshes `updated`; add `agent`/`model`/`session`
   when available). Study the task's objectives in the `## Design` section (fill
   it from the plan and analysis if missing). Log the take-in in the worklog.
3. **Execute** — work the phase section's items top to bottom; update markers as
   you go. Any deviation goes back into this file or the plan — never silently.
4. **Complete** — `[x]` every item of the phase, run the **verify-step** below,
   then move to the next phase; set the file `completed` (the `## Review` block
   is written file-level) and update the linked plan.
5. **Commit gate** — see `instructions/git.md` for the procedure, the message
   format and the approval gate.

**Durable ledger.** Conversation memory does not survive compaction. Keep the
record of progress in durable files — this task file, the plan and the worklog —
not only in short-lived todos; narration between steps is at most one short
line.

**Batched reads.** Read for breadth, not one file at a time:

- **Batch what a single command can return** (one `list`, one `search` over the
  whole corpus) instead of a call per candidate; several files the same question
  needs come back in one round.
- **Broad exploration goes to a sub-agent.** When a phase needs the surrounding
  code or corpus surveyed, dispatch a bounded exploration to a sub-agent and take
  back its findings: the bulk never enters this context, and the sub-agent's
  answer is a pointer plus the load-bearing lines.
- **Keep the original retrievable.** A batched or summarised result names its
  files so they can be re-read exactly (`development.md`, *Context economy*).
- When this does not apply: a phase that reads two known files needs no batch.

**Re-inject the hard rule at a boundary.** A rule that matters in every phase
loses force when a long execution has pushed it out of the recent window. Restate
it at each phase boundary — at take-in, and when a phase closes — in one line,
pointing at the owning contract rather than restating it. When this does not
apply: a phase short enough to hold it needs no re-injection.

**The replacement note.** When a long task loses its working state — a
compaction, a context switch, a fresh session — the next reader needs a note with
fixed parts, not a narration:

| Part | Carries |
|------|---------|
| Goal | what this work is for, in one line |
| Files | the paths already touched and their state |
| Decisions | each ruling and its reason |
| Next | the exact next step, not a summary of the plan |
| Failed attempts | every approach tried that did not work, **and why it failed** |

- The failed-attempts row is the part that pays: an approach recorded without its
  reason is repeated, because the next reader cannot tell a dead end from a gap.
- Write it into the task file, the plan or the worklog — a place that survives the
  session — never into the conversation alone.

**Rulings, not stalls.** Inside an approved plan, settle execution-level details
the plan already authorizes and record each as
`Ruling: <what was decided> — <why> — <cost if wrong>` in the task file or
worklog, then keep going. A decision that changes scope, style, architecture or
cost is not a ruling — it is a question for the user (HARD RULE 7).

### The `>execute` trigger

`>execute` is the **workflow** command that drives this file. Its subject is a
`plan` and one of that plan's task files; it resolves the subject through the
working-context ladder (`context/commands/index.md`, *Working-context
resolution*) — explicit payload → exactly one active plan with an unfinished
task file → the plan under discussion → **ask**. Then:

- **Pick** — select the task file with no pending dependency; if several are
  eligible, apply the ladder and **ask** when it is not unique. Work one file at
  a time.
- **Take in** — `sdt context task wip <id> --phase <n>` before any work (step 2).
- **Execute / complete** — steps 3-4, including the verify-step.
- **Stop conditions** — exactly four; never invent work:
  - an operation the workflow reserves for the user — a commit, an AGENTS.md
    write, or any irreversible, destructive or security-sensitive action → stop
    and **ask**;
  - a side effect outside the task's scope — a merge, a push, a publish, or
    touching another work stream's files → stop and **ask**;
  - a broken or missing plan — no task file (point to `>tasks`) or a plan where
    every path forward is a guess → stop and **ask**;
  - an ambiguous or already-closed subject — more than one candidate plan/task,
    or a `completed` plan (do not reopen it silently) → list the candidates and
    **ask**.

## Multi-session handoff

Single-session work needs none of this; apply it only when an effort is larger
than one session or a second agent may run concurrently.

- **Claim before working.** Before taking a task file, claim it so a concurrent
  session can see the work is taken (`sdt context task claim --plan <plan>
  --agent <id>`). Never start a task file that is already claimed or in progress.
- **The frontier is the claimable work.** `sdt context resume` lists the
  **frontier** — the task files that are pending, unblocked and unclaimed. Pick
  from the frontier, never an arbitrary file.
- **Release when you stop.** If you abandon a claim, release it
  (`sdt context task release`); a claim left past the stale window shows in the
  frontier as a **stale claim** to release, not to overwrite.
- **The map stays a map.** The plan holds the destination and the decisions; the
  detail lives in the task file, and a claim is durable frontmatter state the CLI
  writes — never hand-edit it.

## Stale task files

At the start of any execution pass, run `sdt context resume` (it reads the
recorded execution state and names stale `in-progress` files) and scan
`context/tasks/` for stale files: status
`in-progress` (or legacy `active`) with `[~]` items that went unupdated for a
long time (compare each file's `updated` with the current date and the recorded
take-in). For each stale file:

1. Receive it and adjust status: locate the exact point of work from
   `updated` and the `[~]` markers.
2. Verify against the linked plan and worklog whether work continued elsewhere
   or stalled.
3. If stalled: reset in-progress items to `[ ]` (todo) or close them
   `[x]` when actually finished; clear all `[~]`; refresh
   `updated`.
4. If the phase is finished but not closed: mark items `[x]`, set the file
   `completed`, update the plan, note it in the worklog.

Never leave `[~]` in-progress markers unattended across sessions, and never
archive a stale task file to hide it: `completed` (or `pending` after a reset)
is the normal terminal state, and archiving is reserved for a task file the user
explicitly retires.

## Deviations

When the work departs from what the plan said, record it in the task file's
`## Deviations` section instead of silently reinterpreting the plan. Write it with

```bash
sdt context task deviation add "<what departed from the plan>" --kind <kind> \
  [--id <checklist-id>] [--reason "<why>"]
```

The kind is a closed vocabulary and is **never inferred**:

| Kind | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `fix` | the plan was wrong and the correction is made |
| `add` | something had to be added that the plan did not have |
| `unblock` | a blocker was resolved to continue |
| `stop-and-ask` | the work halted for the user's decision |

`stop-and-ask` is the **default**: when the caller does not say what kind of
diversion this is, the recorded instruction is to ask the user, because "when in
doubt, take the heavier path, and never weaken an agreed plan mid-task without
saying so". An unknown kind is refused by the CLI and reported by
`sdt context lint`, so the vocabulary cannot drift by hand-editing.

With `--id <checklist-id>` the item the deviation concerns is **marked, never
deleted**: `[!]` (stopped to ask) for `stop-and-ask`, `[~]` (in progress)
otherwise. Its text and id stay, so a reviewer reads what was planned next to why
it moved. `sdt context task deviation list` lists the section; an item is closed
by ticking it like any other checklist item. A `completed` file that still
carries an open deviation is a lint WARNING.

## Verify-step

Run the verify-step before closing a phase (completeness, coherence,
correctness) and use the standardized claim vocabulary (**passed** / **expected**
/ **inferred**) — see AGENTS.md (5-stage development lifecycle).

Also run the **prose self-check** on any document the phase returns — no recap
ending, no filler, every specific fact preserved
(`context/instructions/writing.md`).

**No completion claim without fresh evidence.** Before writing a status as done,
run the check now and read its result: identify what proves the claim, run it,
read the output, verify it against the claim, then write the claim. Evidence
from an earlier run does not substitute for a fresh one.

Match each claim to the evidence it needs:

| Claim | Required evidence |
|-------|-------------------|
| passed | the command output from this run |
| expected | the artefact was written and inspected, not executed |
| inferred | a static reading only (file, diff, type) — say so |

Two rules bound what the evidence may conclude:

- **Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.** A search that finds
  nothing proves only that the search found nothing: state the search and its
  scope, and mark the conclusion UNVERIFIED.
- **A schema-valid artefact is not a complete one.** Passing a structural check
  does not establish coverage; pair it with a declared coverage check or record
  coverage as unproven.
- **A "diff confined to this change" audit is scoped to the files the task
  owns.** Under a concurrent stream the whole-tree diff is not a valid
  confinement check — state the owned file set and audit against it.
- **Scope per-edit validation to the files touched.** The full suite runs in the
  plan's Validation phase; a per-edit run of everything is slow and can hang
  offline (see `instructions/development.md`).

Record it as a file-level `## Review` block in the task file (write it with
`sdt context task review --phase <n>`; a completed file without the block gets
a lint SUGGESTION). Every finding ends as one of the closed verdicts
**CONFIRMED**, **DISPROVED** or **UNVERIFIED**, with evidence (command output,
file path). Findings are validated in an **independent pass**; the author of the
phase does not self-approve.

- **An independent opinion needs a separate context.** The independent pass must
  come from a **different model or host** with no shared working context — a
  second pass by the same agent that wrote the work is not independent. When no
  second model/host is available, record **"no independent check was possible"**
  as the verdict and treat the review as **degraded**; never self-approve
  silently.

- **Review completeness, not the chosen direction.** A verdict judges whether the
  deliverable is complete and evidenced against its claims; it does not re-open a
  decision already taken in the analysis. A direction concern is a new analysis
  or an open question, never a review verdict.

### Disposition — what kind of gap the review found

A **verdict** says whether a claim held; the **disposition** says why a review
that ran did not settle the thing. It is the closed three-value vocabulary, one
per review, written under `## Review` as a `### Disposition` subsection:

| Disposition | When |
|-------------|------|
| `CONTEXT-GAP` | the material the review needed was not available — the file was not written yet, a dependency had not landed, the diff was not reachable. The next step is to produce the missing context, not to redo the judgement. |
| `JUDGMENT-GAP` | the material was available and the call made on it was wrong, or a claim was made that the evidence does not carry. The next step is a new judgement, with the evidence named. |
| `NONE` | the review found no gap of either kind. |

The two are not interchangeable: a CONTEXT-GAP reported as a fix produces work on
a wrong premise, and a JUDGMENT-GAP reported as "waiting on context" hides a
defect behind an excuse. When the independent pass is impossible
(**"no independent check was possible"** above), the review is degraded and the
disposition is `CONTEXT-GAP` — the missing context is the missing reviewer.

Give each finding its own identity with `--finding "<claim>" --verdict <verdict>`
(repeatable, paired by position): the pairs become one item per finding under the
block's `### Findings` subsection, with a CLI-assigned id, so a review says
*which* claim was judged rather than leaving verdicts in a blob of prose. The
verdict is the item's trailing token — that is what lint reads — and re-recording
the same claim with the same verdict is a no-op, so a finding's identity is
stable across a re-run. A claim re-stated with a *different* verdict is a new
finding with a new id; the earlier one is never overwritten.

The delivery gate leaves its own evidence: `sdt agent gate --record --plan <ref>`
appends a `### Gate` subsection under the same `## Review` block, one line per
step with its status and duration, headed by the run timestamp. A run that failed
is written with the step it failed at and that step's real status — never
summarised as a pass. Without `--record` the gate writes nothing.

Close-out checks, run before setting the file `completed`:

- `sdt context reindex` and `sdt context lint` — index regenerated, no new
  findings.
- `sdt context resume` — read the recorded execution state; no stale
  `in-progress` file left behind.
- `sdt context docs` — regenerate the per-command docs when the command help or
  the version changed (see `context/sdtdocs/README.md`).
